UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>Actuarial science will prepare you for a career as an actuary. To provide professional actuarial advice in Australia, and in most other developed countries, you must become a fellow of the local actuarial profession. This involves developing basic technical skills, learning how to apply these skills in a general setting, developing deep specialist knowledge and completing a professional course following several years'' relevant work experience. You will have the opportunity to gain industry-based experience as part of the specialisations in this course: Actuarial studies and Actuarial analytics. You will be able to seek employment in international finance and business fields dealing with life, general or health insurance, superannuation, risk management and financial consulting. Many actuaries and actuarial graduates work on investment operations for asset managers, brokers and banks. There are a range of government-related jobs in regulatory control, workers'' compensation, education and others. Some actuaries work with large companies on long-term strategic planning.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The Bachelor of Actuarial Science is a specialist course, structured in four parts: Part A. Core studies, Part B. Discipline studies, Part C. Specialist studies and Part D. Elective studies.<br/><br/><strong>Part A. Core studies<br/></strong><br/>These units will provide you with a comprehensive study of economics and econometrics disciplines and the impact they have on multi-discipline decision-making in organisations. The units consider the impact on the business, professional and public policy communities.<br/><br/><strong>Part B. Discipline studies</strong></p> <p>These units will develop your capacity as a critical and creative professional who is able to apply your knowledge of actuarial science to provide discipline-based solutions to commerce. Units contained within the discipline may meet the requirements for professional accreditation bodies.</p> <p><strong>Part C. Specialist studies</strong></p> <p>These units let you focus more deeply on advanced areas of actuarial science. They develop your expertise in applying specialised knowledge and approaches to complex commerce problems, preparing you for professional practice or further study. Units contained within this component may meet the requirements for professional accreditation bodies. In this part you can select your area of interest, either actuarial studies or actuarial analytics. You will also consolidate your knowledge and skills acquired through the completion of a capstone unit as part of your specialist studies.</p> <p><strong>Part D. Elective studies</strong><br/><br/>These studies will enable you to further develop your knowledge of actuarial science, or commerce more broadly, or to select any units from across the University in which you are eligible to enrol, including to complete a major or minor from another course. </p> <p><strong>Masters Accelerated Pathway</strong></p> <p>Students who are progressing through Parts A, B, and C at a standard required for Actuaries Institute exemptions can use 24 credit points of the 48 credit points to complete four Level 5 units selected from B6060 Stage 1 Part D Application Studies, Additional actuarial studies units as an accelerated pathway into the Master of Actuarial Studies, subject to Faculty approval. Students who then successfully complete the Bachelor of Actuarial Science to the required standard will be eligible for admission into the Master of Actuarial Studies at entry level 3.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/business/current-students/course-advice-and-planning/helpful-links/course-maps/2026/undergraduate">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 156,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>IELTS (Academic): 6.5 overall (no band lower than 6.0)</li><li>Pearson Test of English (Academic): 58 overall with no band lower than 50</li><li>TOEFL Internet-based test: 79 overall with minimum scores: Writing: 21, Listening: 12, Reading: 13, Speaking: 18</li><li>Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/B2033',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '097257F';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>This course will prepare you for a career as an actuary. To provide professional actuarial advice in Australia, and in most other developed countries, you must become a Fellow of the actuarial profession. This involves developing basic technical skills, learning how to apply these skills in a general setting, developing deep specialist knowledge and completing several years of work experience. This double degree will allow you to complete the Foundation Program and the university component of the Actuary Program under the Accreditation Agreement with the Actuaries Institute and is designed for getting the maximum number of exemptions within the shortest time. You will have the opportunity to gain industry-based experience as part of the specialisations in the bachelor component of this course: Actuarial studies and Actuarial analytics. You will be able to seek employment in international finance and business fields dealing with life, general or health insurance, superannuation, risk management and financial consulting. Many actuaries and actuarial graduates work on investment operations for asset managers, brokers and banks. There are a range of government-related jobs in regulatory control, workers'' compensation, education and others. Some actuaries work with large companies on long-term strategic planning.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>Double degree courses include the core features of the component degree courses.</p> <p><strong>Stage 1. Bachelor of Actuarial Science<br/></strong><br/>This course is a specialist course, structured in four parts. In this double degree course you complete: </p> <p><strong>Part A. Core studies<br/></strong><br/>These units will provide you with a comprehensive study of economics and econometrics disciplines and the impact they have on multi-discipline decision-making in organisations. The units consider the impact on the business, professional and public policy communities.<br/><br/><strong>Part B. Discipline studies </strong><br/><br/>These units will develop your capacity as a critical and creative professional who is able to apply your knowledge of actuarial science to provide discipline based solutions to commerce. Units contained within the discipline may meet the requirements for professional accreditation bodies.</p> <p><strong>Part C. Specialist studies</strong></p> <p>These units let you focus more deeply on advanced areas of actuarial science. They develop your expertise in applying specialised knowledge and approaches to complex commerce problems, preparing you for professional practice or further study. Units contained within this component may meet the requirements for professional accreditation bodies. In this part you can select your area of interest, either actuarial studies or actuarial analytics. You will also consolidate your knowledge and skills acquired through the completion of a capstone unit as part of your specialist studies.<br/><br/><strong>Part D. Elective studies</strong><br/><br/>These studies will enable you to further develop your knowledge of actuarial science, or commerce more broadly, or to select any units from across the University in which you are eligible to enrol including to complete a major or minor from another course.</p> <p>You can enrich your degree to hone your academic and professional skills with a range of flagship rich experiences, including the <a href="https://www.monash.edu/research-experimentation-discovery">Research, Experimentation and Discovery</a>, <a href="https://www.monash.edu/innovation-guarantee">Monash Innovation Guarantee</a> and<a href="https://www.monash.edu/study-abroad/outbound/global-immersion-guarantee?SQ_VARIATION_2808038=0"> Global Immersion Guarantee</a>. These flagship rich experience units may be credited in place of your elective studies if approved by the faculty. There are both 6 and 12 credit point unit options available.</p> <p><strong>Stage 2. Master of Actuarial Studies</strong></p> <p>The course is structured in four parts: Part A. Advanced preparatory studies, Part B. Core studies, Part C. Discipline studies and Part D. Elective studies. In the double degree course you complete: </p> <p><strong>Part B. Core studies</strong><br/><br/>These units will develop your understanding of actuarial, statistical, and econometric methods and will enable you to provide discipline based solutions within a business, professional or public policy community.</p> <p><strong> Part C. Disciplines studies</strong></p> <p>These units will develop your capacity as a critical and creative professional who is able to apply your knowledge of actuarial studies to provide discipline based solutions to commerce. <br/><br/><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/business/current-students/course-advice-and-planning/helpful-links/course-maps/2026/undergraduate">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>Other</strong></td><td>Articulation agreements: Beijing Jiaotong University (BJTU) (2+2) *Approval in progress (Refer to B2033)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level A: IELTS Academic 6.5 overall (no band lower than 6.0)</li><li>Monash Level A: Pearson Test of English Academic 58 overall (no band lower than 50)</li><li>Monash Level A: TOEFL Internet-based test 79 overall (Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18)</li><li>Monash Level A: Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/B6060',
