UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>Economics is the science of allocating scarce resources to achieve desired ends. It has evolved into a broad discipline that helps us understand historical trends, interpret current events, and make reasoned predictions about the future. Economists study the incentives that shape the decisions individuals, businesses and governments, and the organisation and functioning of societies. Modern economic analysis draws upon insights from other social sciences and technological advances in data analysis to develop creative solutions for local and global problems.</p> <p>In this program, you will choose one of four specialisations that are designed to provide deeper knowledge and expertise. Each specialisation is designed to encourage logical thought and detailed analysis of socio-economic questions that will be valuable in a range of careers.</p> <p><strong>SPECIALISATIONS AVAILABLE:</strong></p> <ul> <li>Economics and economic policy</li> <li>Business analytics for economics</li> <li>Macroeconomics and financial markets</li> <li>Mathematical economics and econometrics</li> </ul><h4>Structure</h4><p>This course comprises four parts: Part A. Core studies, Part B. Specialist studies, Part C Capstone studies, and Part D. Elective studies.<br/><br/><strong>Part A. Core studies</strong></p> <p>These units will provide you with a comprehensive study of economics and econometrics disciplines and the impact they have on multi-discipline decision-making in organisations. The units consider the impact on the business, professional and public policy communities.<br/><br/><strong>Part B. Specialist studies</strong></p> <p>These units will develop your capacity as a critical and creative professional who is able to apply your knowledge of a specialised area to provide discipline based solutions to commerce and economic policy.</p> <p><strong>Part C. Capstone studies</strong></p> <p>The capstone unit is designed to consolidate the knowledge and skills acquired throughout the study of your course and provide multi-disciplinary processes and decision making.<br/><br/><strong>Part D. Elective studies</strong><br/><br/>This will enable you to further develop your knowledge of economics or commerce more broadly, or select any units from across the University in which you are eligible to enrol including to complete a major or minor from another course.</p> <p><strong>Masters Accelerated Pathway</strong></p> <p>If you have completed at least 96 credit points and have achieved a high credit (65%) average, you can apply to the Course Coordinator for the Master’s accelerated pathway. If admitted to the accelerated pathway, you will use four of your elective studies (from Part D) to complete 24 credit points of specified units from Master of Economics (B6030) (Part B).</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/business/current-students/course-advice-and-planning/helpful-links/course-maps/2026/undergraduate">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 156,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<h4>Entry Requirements</h4><p>Monash College - Diploma of Commerce<br/>Monash College - Diploma of Business</p> <p><strong>Articulation agreements:</strong></p> <p>Abu Dhabi University - 2.5+1.5 - Exp: 12/07/2025<br/>Amity University (Noida campus) - 2+2, (1.5+1.5), (1+2) - Exp: 01/07/2025<br/>Beijing Foreign Studies University (BFSU) - 2+2 - Exp: 19/05/2024 renewal in progress<br/>Beijing Institute of Technology, Zhuhai (BITZH) - 2+2 - Exp: 19/05/2024 renewal in progress<br/>Beijing Jiaotong University (BJTU) - 2+2 - *Approval in progress<br/>Central South University (CSU) - 2+2 - Exp: 25/11/2025<br/>Central University of Finance and Economics (CUFE) - 2+2 - Exp: 16/06/2024 renewal in progress<br/>Diplomatic Academy of Vietnam (DAV) - 1+2 - Exp: 27/06/2027<br/>Dongbei University of Finance and Economics (DUFE) - 2+2 - Exp: 11/11/2024<br/>Guangdong University of Finance (GDUF) - 2+2 - Exp: 12/12/2024<br/>Guangdong University of Technology (GDUT) - 2+2 - Exp: 16/04/2028<br/>Hanoi University of Science and Technology (HUST) - 2.5+1.5 - Exp: 07/08/2026<br/>Henan University - 2+2 - Exp: 17/04/24 renewal in progress<br/>Hunan University - 2+2 - Exp: 19/07/2026<br/>International University, Vietnam National University (IU VNU HCMC) - 1.5+1.5 - Exp: 12/12/2026<br/>Narsee Monjee Institute of Management Studies (NMIMS) - 1.5+1.5 - Exp: 05/09/2028<br/>O.P Jindal Global University - 1.5+1.5 - Exp: 16/05/2027<br/>Shanghai International Studies University (SISU) - 1.5+2 - Exp: 13/01/2027<br/>Shanghai University of Finance and Economics (SUFE) - 1.5+2 - Exp: 30/09/2026<br/>South China Agricultural University (SCAU) - 2+2 - Exp: 23/05/2027<br/>Southwestern University of Finance and Economics (SWUFE) - 2+2 - Exp: 28/02/2027<br/>University of Danang (DUE - UE) - 2+2 - Exp: 12/02/2028<br/>University of Danang (DUE - VNUK) - 2+2 - Exp: 17/08/2025<br/>University of Economics and Business, Vietnam National University (UEB-VNU) - 2+2 - Exp: 29/08/2028<br/>University of Economics, International School of Business, Ho Chi Minh City (UEH-ISB) - 1.5+1.5 &amp; 1+2 - Exp: 29/11/2027<br/>Vidyalankar School of Information Technology (VSIT) - (1.5+2), (2+1.5), (2+2) - Exp: 31/01/2026<br/>Xi''an International Studies University (XISU) - 2+2 - Exp: 30/05/2029<br/>Xi''an Jiaotong-Liverpool University (XJTLU) - 2+2 - Exp: 30/06/2029<br/>Zhengzhou University (ZZU) - 2+2 - Exp: 25/07/2026</p><h4>English Language Requirements</h4><p>Monash Level A, that is: IELTS (Academic): 6.5 overall (no band lower than 6.0); or Pearson Test of English (Academic): score of 58 overall with no band lower than 50; or TOEFL Internet-based test: score of 79 overall with minimum scores: Writing: 21, Listening: 12, Reading: 13 and Speaking: 18; or Equivalent approved English testt</p>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/B2031',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '001447E';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>The Bachelor of Education (Honours) course will prepare you for a career as a skilled, critical and effective teacher and educator in the early childhood, primary and secondary sectors, or a combination of these.</p> <p>You will build a high level of expertise in one of seven specialisations:</p> <ul> <li>Early childhood and primary education</li> <li>Primary education (single degree and double degree course)</li> <li>Primary and secondary education</li> <li>Primary and secondary health and physical education</li> <li>Primary and secondary inclusive and special education</li> <li>Secondary health and physical education</li> <li>Secondary education (double degree only).</li> </ul> <p>The course will prepare you for teaching in an increasingly diverse, complex and changing world. You will engage with contemporary ideas and practices and learn how to foster the learning of children and young people with varied needs. This will enable you to develop your students'' creative, intellectual, physical, personal and social capabilities.</p> <p>While learning to navigate the complex nature of teaching, you will develop the specific professional knowledge and teaching skills needed to engage students of various ages and developmental stages with particular school subjects. Depending upon your specialisation, you will develop the skills and knowledge needed to teach in learning areas such as the arts and music, English, mathematics, humanities, physical education, health and wellbeing, science, and digital technologies. Professional experience offered throughout the course in urban, rural, remote or international locations and education settings, will build your knowledge of teaching and learning. As a result, you will become a thoughtful, effective and inclusive educator, able to design learning experiences that ensure that your future students have the right balance of challenge and support.</p> <p>The Bachelor of Education (Honours) prepares you for the global workforce. You will have a strong foundation for decision making about your practice and you will be in a position to establish yourself as an educational leader of the future.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course develops through the five themes of Part A. Core studies, Part B. Education studies, Part C. Curriculum studies, Part D. Discipline studies and Part E. Professional experience. The nature and proportion of units required among these themes will differ depending upon your specialisation and discipline studies.</p> <p><strong>Part A. Core studies</strong></p> <p>The core studies include essential units that are part of every Bachelor of Education (Honours) specialisation. These units are designed to ground you in evidence-based practices, preparing you to navigate diverse classroom environments and enhance student learning effectively. A key focus of this theme is developing your knowledge and skills to choose strategies that support students to learn, retain, and apply knowledge effectively.</p> <p>You will explore effective teaching practices, such as sequencing for learning, modelling expected outcomes, assessing and reporting student progress, and providing constructive feedback to support student growth. Classroom management is another major component where you will learn to create structured, supportive environments using clear rules, routines, and high expectations. You will also develop skills to build positive relationships with students, engaging them actively in their learning journey. Inclusivity is embedded throughout the units and you will gain foundational knowledge of First Nations cultures and perspectives, along with other enabling factors that promote cultural responsiveness and support for diverse learners. These units will equip you to become an adaptable, culturally aware, and skilled teacher, ready to apply evidence-based practices for impactful and inclusive teaching and learning.</p> <p><strong>Part </strong><strong>B</strong><strong>. Education studies</strong> </p> <p>The units in this sequence cover essential aspects of learning to teach and are tailored to each specialisation to prepare you appropriately to enter the profession in your chosen sector (early childhood, primary, secondary, or a combination of these). These studies include an examination of the learning and teaching relationship, child and/or adolescent development, alongside classroom culture and practice. You will build your professional skills, knowledge and understanding of teaching through a process of personal inquiry into effective teaching practices, informed by current research. These units will challenge you to consider global and local influences on education as well as the effective and innovative incorporation of emerging technologies to ensure the relevance of your teaching. You will build a deep understanding of learners, families, schools and communities. Societal trends are explored as well as how to effectively engage local communities, parents and carers.</p> <p><strong>Part </strong><strong>C</strong><strong>. Curriculum studies</strong></p> <p>With a focus on the Victorian Curriculum, these units will provide you with the content-specific knowledge pedagogies, covering the early years framework, Foundation–10 curriculum, as well as specific pedagogy appropriate for teaching VCE in a particular discipline or disciplines. You will develop the understanding and skills needed to become a skilled, innovative and effective educator who is able to draw on a repertoire of strategies to suit particular students, educational contexts and learning outcomes. You will learn how to design, plan and implement engaging learning experiences to meet diverse learners'' needs. You will develop your knowledge and skills in key learning areas relevant to the educational level of your specialisation, deepening your understanding of teaching and learning, assessment strategies, theories and practices related to the subjects you will be teaching.</p> <p>If you are enrolled in the Bachelor of Education (Honours) in Secondary Health and Physical Education, you will be prepared to teach in three secondary teaching areas: health, physical education and one other area of your choice.</p> <p>If you are enrolled in a primary/secondary specialisation, you will be prepared to teach in one secondary teaching area as well as the curriculum areas required for teaching in primary schools.</p> <p>If you are enrolled in a Bachelor of Education (Honours) in Secondary Education double degree course, you will be prepared to teach in two secondary teaching areas. You will also be required to successfully complete a unit that develops skills in teaching literacy and numeracy across the curriculum, ensuring you have the skills and expertise to share in the responsibility of educating literate and numerate learners.</p> <p><strong>Part </strong><strong>D</strong><strong>. Discipline studies</strong></p> <p>These studies units are designed to develop your content knowledge in the key learning areas that you will be teaching in schools or other education settings. These units are offered as integrated curriculum/discipline studies in the early childhood and primary, and primary specialisations, and as a standalone discipline studies sequence in all other specialisations. They will provide you with a focused sequence of study that will deepen and advance your knowledge in your teaching specialist areas. The number of units completed in this sequence is unique to each specialisation and accordingly aligns and reflects the different needs of the early childhood, primary and secondary school sectors.</p> <p><strong>Part </strong><strong>E</strong><strong>. Professional experience </strong></p> <p>Integral to the course, the professional experience opportunities have been designed as a developmental learning sequence that you will engage with at every stage of your course. This component is an opportunity for you to build connections between the core, education, curriculum and discipline themed unit content and apply these in a professional context. You will operate within a particular social and legislative environment unique to your placement setting, and interact with sector professionals as you work collaboratively with them whilst on placement. An opportunity to further explore these professional elements is facilitated through the Teaching Performance Assessment (TPA) unit.</p> <p><strong>Course progression maps</strong></p> <p> The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/education/students/courses/maps/2026">course progression maps</a> provide guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong><label></strong></td><td><ul><li><point></li><li><point></li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/D3001',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '082360G';

