UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>The Bachelor of Media Communication responds to the increasing global demand for graduates with a deep understanding of the role of media in contemporary social, cultural and economic life and well-developed professional skills in communication. The course will provide you with analytical and critical thinking skills through a core program of professional communication practice alongside specialist study in a related field such as journalism, media, screen and public relations. The core units will offer a range of practical experiences utilising the Monash Media Lab.</p> <p>You will develop cross-cultural competency, social awareness and a global outlook. This course is suitable if you wish to pursue a career in media communication occupations across a broad range of media industry sectors and other professional domains, including government, corporate and NGO. Relevant occupations include journalist, video producer, media content coordinator, public relations professional, digital communications coordinator, director of social media, researcher, media educator.</p> <strong><strong>The Bachelor of Media Communication offer four specialisations:</strong></strong> <ul> <li>Journalism</li> <li>Media</li> <li>Public relations </li> <li>Screen</li> </ul> <p>You will also have the opportunity to enrich your degree by honing your academic and professional skills through a range of <a href="https://www.monash.edu/flagship-rich-experiences">flagship rich educational experiences (FREE)</a>. For more information on eligibility please see <a href="https://www.monash.edu/flagship-rich-experiences">FREE</a>.</p> <h4>Structure</h4><p>The course is structured in four parts: Part A. Core studies, Part B. Foundation studies, Part C. Specialist studies and Part D. Free elective studies.</p> <p><strong>Part A. Core studies</strong></p> <p><strong>These units build your competence in:</strong></p> <ul><li>analysing and understanding the current state and past developments in media communication locally and globally;</li><li>understanding impacts of media and communication transformation, and media power; and</li><li>creating future scenarios and media communication visions by developing your preparedness and adaptability for future media communication change and creativity.</li></ul> <p><strong>Part B. Foundation studies</strong></p> <p>These units involve developing your competencies across a range of specified media areas and linking knowledge and media practice in professional contexts, such as conceptual and implementation skills, project and content management skills, hands-on experience in putting knowledge into practice, and thereby testing and creatively experimenting with (strategic) knowledge about media transitions and transformations. It also involves developing your competency in ethical and legal aspects of media communication.</p> <p><strong>Part C</strong>. <strong>Specialist studies</strong></p> <p>These units involve further developing specialised competencies necessary for you to work in different knowledge communities and media communication professional settings. It includes competence in engaging with stakeholders, working in teams, media literacy, and participatory research and media making in collaboration with experts from academia, industry, government, and the community. It also includes competency in social and cross-cultural awareness.</p> <p><strong>Part D. Free elective studies</strong></p> <p>Free elective units enable you to further your knowledge of media communication more broadly, or to select units from across the faculty or University in which you are eligible to enrol. If you are in a double degree course, some units required for the other degree can also be credited as electives towards the media communication specialist degree. You can also use your free electives for study tours, field trips, internships, exchange opportunities or explore the <a href="https://www.monash.edu/students/handbooks/faculty-info/undergrad/arts/arts-enrichment-units">Arts enrichment </a>and <a href="https://www.monash.edu/students/handbooks/faculty-info/undergrad/arts/monash-intercultural-lab-units">Monash Intercultural Lab</a> for other available options.</p> <p><strong>Bachelor + Masters accelerated pathway</strong></p> <p>If you satisfy the below requirements, you may be invited to join the master''s accelerated pathway program:</p> <ul><li>have achieved a minimum Weighted Average Mark (WAM) of 65%; and </li><li>have completed a minimum of 96 credit points in your bachelor degree, including 24 credit points towards your Arts specialisation.</li></ul> <p>Please note that you should also have a minimum of 24 credit points of free electives available.</p> <p>On joining the accelerated pathway your 24 credit points of free elective units will be replaced with specified units from your destination masters degree. As long as you attain a minimum WAM of 60% at the end of your bachelor studies, you will be eligible to continue your chosen masters studies the following year.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/arts/course-information/maps/">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 156,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>IELTS (Academic): 6.5 overall (no band lower than 6.0)</li><li>Pearson Test of English (Academic): 58 overall with no band lower than 50</li><li>TOEFL iBT: 79 overall (Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18)</li><li>Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/A2002',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '093854F';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>The double degree Bachelor of Media Communication and Bachelor of Music is designed to meet the evolving demands of contemporary careers in both the media and music industries. This unique interdisciplinary approach allows you to harness the power of media and communication alongside developing expert musical skills, preparing for a range of dynamic and influential roles in today’s global landscape.</p> <p>The Bachelor of Media Communication degree responds to the global demand for skilled professionals with a deep understanding of media’s role in society, cultural and economic life and advanced communication skills. You will engage in critical analysis, develop professional communication practices, and choose from specialisations in Journalism, Media, Public Relations, or Screen. Core coursework emphasises practical experience through the Monash Media Lab, fostering both analytical and practical skills. The curriculum is designed to build cross-cultural competency and social awareness, equipping you for diverse roles such as journalists, media content coordinators, screen creators, public relations professionals, and more.</p> <p>The Bachelor of Music focuses on developing high-level performance skills and a deep understanding of music theory, history, and context. You will be immersed in a high-impact learning and teaching that supports you to develop core competencies in practice and performance, theory and analysis, and history and context. Specialisations allow you to tailor your studies according to your interests and career goals, supported by opportunities in mobility programs and professional development.</p> <p>By undertaking this double degree, you will benefit from a comprehensive education that champions both creative and communicative excellence. The combination encourages a synthesis of creative expression with media savvy, ideal for careers in entertainment journalism, digital content creation for music and arts organisations, public relations for cultural events, and roles that require both media and musical expertise. You will also have the opportunity to participate in flagship rich educational experiences, enhancing both your academic and professional aspirations.</p> <h4>Structure</h4><p>Double degree courses include the features of the component degree courses, except electives that may be reduced.</p> <p><strong>Bachelor of Media Communication</strong></p> <p>The Bachelor of Media Communication is a specialist course, structured in four parts. In the double degree you complete:</p> <p><strong>Part A. Core studies</strong></p> <p><strong>These units build your competence in:</strong></p> <ul><li>analysing and understanding the current state and past developments in media communication locally and globally;</li><li> understanding impacts of media and communication transformation, and media power; </li><li>creating future scenarios and media communication visions by developing your preparedness and adaptability for future media communication change and creativity.</li></ul> <p><strong>Part B. Foundation studies</strong></p> <p>This involves developing your competencies in linking knowledge and media practice in professional contexts, such as conceptual and implementation skills, project and content management skills, hands-on experience in putting knowledge into practice, and thereby testing and creatively experimenting with (strategic) knowledge about media transitions and transformations. It also involves developing your competency in ethical and legal aspects of media communication. </p> <p><strong>Part C. Specialist studies</strong></p> <p>This involves competencies necessary for you to work in teams and in different knowledge communities and media communication professional settings. It includes competence in engaging with stakeholders, media literacy, and participatory research and media making in collaboration with experts from academia, industry, government, and the community. It also includes competency in social and cross-cultural awareness.</p> <p><strong>Bachelor of Music</strong></p> <p>The Bachelor of Music is a specialist course, structured in four parts. In the double degree you complete:</p> <p><strong>Part A. Specialist studies</strong><br/><br/>This sequence of units will provide you with specialist skills to undertake a final examination either through performance with your chief instrument or voice, or through a folio of composition and music technology media or written work. Performance and other applied electives are also available.<br/><br/><strong>Part B. Music theory and ear training</strong><br/><br/>These units will integrate the development of aural skills with the theoretical understanding of music through listening, analysis, performance, notation and composition.<br/><br/><strong>Part C. Context studies </strong><br/><br/>These studies will expose you to the study of music and music making in various cultural, historical, social and professional settings through introductory units and a capstone experience. </p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/arts/course-information/maps">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level A: IELTS (Academic) 6.5 overall, no band lower than 6.0</li><li>Monash Level A: Pearson Test of English (Academic) score 58 overall, no band lower than 50</li><li>Monash Level A: TOEFL iBT score 79 overall with minimum Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18</li><li>Monash Level A: Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/A2021',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '117270F';

