UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>Pharmacists make a difference to people''s health in hospitals, aged-care facilities and local communities. In this course you will learn about how medicines work and their impact on the body. You will also develop the knowledge, skills, and expertise you need to make a difference as a member of the healthcare team.</p> <p>The course is fully integrated across units and themes, using an interactive instructional model that includes lots of hands-on practice opportunities. You will learn from experienced teachers, pharmacists, and researchers and have access to some of the country''s best-equipped facilities at our Parkville campus.</p> <p>You will undertake work placements in hospitals and community pharmacies, applying an essential understanding of how pharmacists contribute medicines expertise in healthcare settings. You will collaborate on a research project that extends your ability to answer questions and question answers in a focused area of inquiry. You will also connect with other health care students – in the classroom and on your placements.</p> <p>You will have access to innovative teaching technologies throughout your studies, such as MyDispense – a web-based pharmacy simulation developed by Monash and adopted by leading pharmacy schools around the world. You will also keep a record of your skills development in an electronic portfolio called COMeT. This portfolio will help you to demonstrate your achievements when seeking employment as a student, an intern, and a pharmacist.</p> <p>Exciting and challenging career options await, including working as a community, hospital or consultant pharmacist, or jobs in the pharmaceutical industry, government, regulatory bodies, research and clinical trials. Our graduates work around the world. A degree in pharmacy from Monash is a degree of opportunity.</p> <p>Our Bachelor of Pharmacy (Honours) Parkville course is accredited by the Australian Pharmacy Council. </p> <p>The Bachelor of Pharmacy (Honours) course is also offered at our Malaysia campus so Australian-based students have the opportunity to complete a one-semester exchange during their bachelor-level studies. There are also exchange opportunities to undertake laboratory, clinical, social, educational, or business research at an approved overseas institution. For students completing their degree at Monash Malaysia, completion of the Australian Pharmacy Council Knowledge Stream (including an eligibility check and passing exams) is also required should students plan to practice in Australia.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course develops through six themes, the first three being structure and function of the body; drug structure, disposition and action; and professional practice, which come together in the final three themes: comprehensive care; inquiry and innovation; and professional experience respectively. This course will develop the relevant practical and analytical skills you require for pharmacy practice, as well as the generic skills such as oral and written communication, critical thinking, inquiry, learning for life, numeracy and information literacy and leadership.</p> <p><strong>Structure and function of the body (how the body works)</strong></p> <p>These studies will provide you with the foundational understanding and knowledge of the human body, including how cells, molecules and organs ensure proper functioning of the body. This will include studies of anatomy, physiology and biochemistry relevant to pharmacists. You will be able to predict the outcome of cell and organ dysfunction, as well as effectively communicate aspects of how the body works.</p> <p><strong>Drug structure, disposition, and action (how medicines work)</strong></p> <p>You will learn the important aspects for a molecule to become a medicine, and how medicines produce their effects. This will include studies of medicinal chemistry, pharmacology, pharmaceutics and pharmacokinetics/pharmacodynamics relevant to pharmacists. These studies will equip you with fundamental and applied knowledge required to understand and assess pre-formulation, formulation, manufacturing and packaging of pharmaceutical products. This includes the understanding and application of how medicines get absorbed, distributed and eliminated from the body and mechanisms and time-course of drug response in the body.</p> <p><strong>Professional practice (what pharmacists do)</strong></p> <p>These studies will equip you with the sociocultural context in which the pharmacist and the client operate, social and health practice issues, ethical, legal context of pharmacy, relevant professional and social values, attitudes and behaviours necessary for practice. You will develop the communication and practical skills to apply the knowledge and skills you have gained to promote and contribute to the quality use of medicines and to the health of the community.</p> <p><strong>Comprehensive care</strong></p> <p>Through these studies you will acquire knowledge about diseases and disorders and their management. You will develop an understanding and application of the relationship between pathophysiology and the rational design and clinical use of drugs in the management of various health conditions. You will gain and apply the knowledge and critical thinking skills in the enabling sciences, pharmaceutics and pharmacy practice to optimise pharmacotherapy in clinical practice.</p> <p><strong>Inquiry and innovation</strong></p> <p>These studies will build upon problem solving and innovative thinking skills developed in previous units. You will apply these skills to plan and conduct a project, think critically, and explore innovative solutions in a laboratory, clinical, social or business environment.</p> <p><strong>Professional experience</strong></p> <p>Embedded practice experiences during the course will prepare you to achieve the necessary competencies to enter the preregistration period if you intend to complete a fifth year. This will include practice experiences in a variety of clinical settings to develop and give context for your skill development.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/students/handbooks/maps/2026/map-p3001.pdf">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level A: IELTS (Academic) 6.5 overall, no band lower than 6.0</li><li>Monash Level A: Pearson Test of English (Academic) score 58 overall, no band lower than 50</li><li>Monash Level A: TOEFL iBT score 79 overall with minimum Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18</li><li>Monash Level A: Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/P3001',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '082370F';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>Monash is the only pharmacy course in Victoria to offer a Bachelor of Pharmacy (Honours) course followed by a Masters of Pharmacy course that is integrated with paid a work-based internship. This means that in the same amount of time it takes to become a pharmacist in any other Australian course, you can extend your practice skills and earn two degrees. The BPharm(Hons)/MPharm vertical double degree has two entry pathways - direct-from-school entry and graduate entry. Either pathway will prepare you for a rewarding and portable career.</p> <p>You will learn about how medicines work and their impact on the body. You will also develop the knowledge, skills, and expertise you need to make a difference as a member of the healthcare team. The course is fully integrated across units and themes, using an interactive instructional model that includes lots of hands-on practice opportunities. You will learn from experienced teachers, pharmacists, and researchers and have access to some of the country''s best-equipped facilities at our Parkville campus.</p> <p>You will undertake work placements in hospitals and community pharmacies, applying an essential understanding of how pharmacists contribute medicines expertise in healthcare settings. You will collaborate on a research project that extends your ability to answer questions and question answers in a focused area of inquiry. You will also connect with other health care students – in the classroom and on your placements.</p> <p>You will have access to innovative teaching technologies throughout your studies, such as MyDispense – a web-based pharmacy simulation developed by Monash and adopted by leading pharmacy schools around the world. You will also keep a record of your skills development in an electronic portfolio called COMeT. This portfolio will help you to demonstrate your achievements when seeking employment as a student, an intern, and a pharmacist.</p> <p>Our Bachelor of Pharmacy (Honours) course is accredited by the Australian Pharmacy Council. To become a registered pharmacist in Australia, you must first complete the Bachelor of Pharmacy (Honours) degree, then undertake a period of supervised practice, while also completing an accredited intern training program. The Monash vertical double degree provides the opportunity to complete workplace learning units along with the accredited intern training program. Together, these contribute credit towards a postgraduate qualification (the Master of Pharmacy). Entry to the master''s component is contingent upon successful completion of the requirements of the Bachelor of Pharmacy (Honours), provisional registration with the Pharmacy Board of Australia and securing an approved intern training position.</p> <p>The Bachelor of Pharmacy (Honours) course is also offered at our Malaysia campus so Australian-based students have the opportunity to complete a one-semester exchange during their bachelor-level studies. There are also exchange opportunities to undertake laboratory, clinical, social, educational, or business research at an approved overseas institution.</p> <p>We acknowledge very high-achieving students by offering access to our scholar''s program in Stage 1 of the course. This provides exclusive learning opportunities, including:</p> <ul> <li>an annual networking event</li> <li>invitations to key Faculty events and seminars</li> <li>a dean''s scholarship (domestic students only)</li> <li>a listing in the annual Faculty Prize Giving Program</li> </ul> <p>Exciting and challenging career options await, including working as a community, hospital or consultant pharmacist, or jobs in the pharmaceutical industry, government, regulatory bodies, research and clinical trials. Our graduates work around the world. A degree in pharmacy from Monash is a degree of opportunity.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course develops through the six themes, the first three being: structure and function of the body; drug structure, disposition and action; and professional practice, which come together in the final three themes: comprehensive care; inquiry and innovation; and professional experience respectively. This course will develop the relevant practical and analytical skills you require for pharmacy practice, as well as your generic skills such as oral and written communication, critical thinking, inquiry, learning for life, numeracy and information literacy and leadership.</p> <p><strong>Structure and function of the body (how the body works)</strong></p> <p>These studies will provide you with the foundational understanding and knowledge of the human body, including how cells, molecules and organs ensure proper functioning of the body. This will include studies of anatomy, physiology and biochemistry relevant to pharmacists. You will be able to predict the outcome of cell and organ dysfunction, as well as effectively communicate aspects of how the body works.</p> <p><strong>Drug structure, disposition, and action (how medicines work)</strong></p> <p>You will learn the important aspects for a molecule to become a medicine, and how medicines produce their effects. This will include studies of medicinal chemistry, pharmacology, pharmaceutics and pharmacokinetics/pharmacodynamics relevant to pharmacists. These studies will equip you with fundamental and applied knowledge required to understand and assess pre-formulation, formulation, manufacturing and packaging of pharmaceutical products. This includes the understanding and application of how medicines get absorbed, distributed and eliminated from the body and mechanisms and time-course of drug response in the body.</p> <p><strong>Professional practice (what pharmacists do)</strong></p> <p>These studies will equip you with the sociocultural context in which the pharmacist and the client operate, social and health practice issues, ethical, legal context of pharmacy, relevant professional and social values, attitudes and behaviours necessary for practice. You will develop the communication and practical skills to apply the knowledge and skills you have gained to promote and contribute to the quality use of medicines and to the health of the community.</p> <p><strong>Comprehensive care</strong></p> <p>Through these studies you will acquire knowledge about diseases and disorders and their management. You will develop an understanding and application of the relationship between pathophysiology and the rational design and clinical use of drugs in the management of various health conditions. You will gain and apply the knowledge and critical thinking skills in the enabling sciences, pharmaceutics and pharmacy practice to optimise pharmacotherapy in clinical practice.</p> <p><strong>Inquiry and innovation</strong></p> <p>These studies will build upon problem-solving and innovative thinking skills developed in previous units. You will apply these skills to plan and conduct a project, think critically, and explore innovative solutions in a laboratory, clinical, social or business environment.</p> <p><strong>Professional experience</strong></p> <p>Embedded practice experiences during stage one will prepare you to achieve the necessary competencies to enter the preregistration period. This will include practice experiences in a variety of clinical settings to develop and give context for your skill development.</p> <p>Once you enter stage two, you will undertake the Pharmacy Board of Australia''s required pre-registration year including the National Alliance for Pharmacy Education intern training program (ITP) accredited by the Australian Pharmacy Council. You will develop your skills in the workplace, focusing on the competencies required to practice as a registered pharmacist. You will also complete the Monash University Pharmacy intern foundation program (IFP) that recognises additional professional knowledge and skills gained whilst working in a Monash credentialed training site.</p> <p>In order to enrol in the intern training program and intern foundation program, you must have arranged to undertake your internship under the supervision of a preceptor approved by the Pharmacy Board of Australia.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/students/handbooks/maps/2026/map-p6001.pdf">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 260,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td>Monash Level A, that is: IELTS (Academic): 6.5 overall (no band lower than 6.0); or Pearson Test of English (Academic): score of 58 overall with no band lower than 50; or TOEFL Internet-based test: score of 79 overall with minimum scores: Writing: 21, Listening: 12, Reading: 13 and Speaking: 18; or Equivalent approved English test</td></tr></tbody></table>',
