UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>In this course, you will develop the relevant knowledge, practical and analytical skills required for speech pathology practice, including extending your skills of communication, ethical and reflective practice, collaboration, research, and leadership. This bachelor (honours) program consists of twenty-eight units delivered over four years. Foundational knowledge and skills are introduced in year one, with the focus on integration of prior science knowledge and principles into the context of speech pathology in additional to the development of clinical skills. The multimodal teaching approaches will facilitate active learning and development, which include early workplace and interprofessional learning opportunities. Clinical placements occur in years three and four, with practice in a simulated setting prior to acute, subacute, community and private practice clinical placements. A learner-centred philosophy allows preferences to be pursued in an independent research project in the final year.</p> <p>This course enables students to obtain an entry-level qualification at an undergraduate (Honours) level of study to practice as a Speech Pathologist, and be practice-ready graduates. The course includes clinical education that satisfies Speech Pathology Australia’s requirements for registration to practice as a Speech Pathologist.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course comprises 192 credit points. The curriculum is delivered over four years and develops across five themes: personal and professional development; population, society and health; fundamental knowledge for working as a speech pathologist within the biopsychosocial model of health sciences; applied practice; and research. These themes are interwoven throughout the course.</p> <p><strong>Personal and professional development</strong><br/>Through this theme you will develop the personal attributes and skills to make the transition to becoming a speech pathologist. These include verbal and written communication skills, reflective practice, ethical and legal knowledge and leadership skills required to practice as a speech pathologist and within an interprofessional team.</p> <p><strong>Population and society</strong> <br/>This theme addresses the broader society and population context and issues that impact upon speech pathology practice, underpinned by an internationally accepted biopsychosocial health model.</p> <p><strong>Fundamental knowledge</strong> <br/>This theme provides the knowledge and skills required for speech pathology practice. It includes contextualisation of previous anatomy, and behavioural sciences knowledge to clinical presentations as well as the theory informing speech pathology, education and leadership practice.</p> <p><strong>Applied practice</strong><br/>This theme addresses clinical competencies for speech pathology practice. It focuses on the incorporation of the best available research evidence with the clinical reasoning skills of assessment, management, evaluation and health care across the lifespan and across a spectrum of educational and health care environments and circumstances.</p> <p><strong>Research</strong><br/>This theme will develop your knowledge and skills to locate, interpret and critically evaluate research relevant to speech pathology practice.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong><br/>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/students/handbooks/maps/2026/map-m3008.pdf">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level C</li><li>IELTS (Academic): 7.0 overall (no band lower than 6.5)</li><li>Pearson Test of English (Academic): score of 65 overall with no band lower than 58</li><li>TOEFL Internet-based test: score of 94 overall with minimum scores: Writing: 24, Listening: 20, Reading: 19 and Speaking: 20</li><li>Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/M3008',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '117403J';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>The Diploma of Art and Design is your first step towards completing your Monash Art, Design or Architecture degree. </p> <p>You''ll learn how to work independently and develop your problem solving, team work and time management skills to prepare you for success at Monash University. You will also enhance your creative thinking, and develop your conceptual and technical skills in this field. This Diploma will also enable you to further develop your portfolio of work.</p> <p>In Part 1 of the course you will develop your personal folio, which will show your creative work.</p> <p>In Part 2 of the course you can choose your area of specialisation to prepare you for your Monash bachelor degree. There are five streams to choose between: Architectural Design, Fine Art, Communication Design, Industrial Design, or Spatial Design.</p> <p>During part 2 you will study the equivalent curriculum and complete the equivalent assessments as first year undergraduate students.</p> <p><strong>Note:</strong> Only students who complete the two trimesters of part 2 will be eligible to receive the diploma qualification.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p><strong>This Diploma course is structured in two parts:</strong></p> <p><strong>Part 1. Foundation knowledge</strong></p> <p>These studies develop your creative skills and understanding of relevant theory and principles, as well as provide the opportunity to develop your personal folio, which will show your creative work. </p> <p><strong>Part 2. Discipline knowledge</strong></p> <p>You will study the equivalent curriculum and complete the assessments that are equivalent to first year undergraduate students. Your classes will help you develop your critical thinking skills and university research techniques. These skills will prepare you for success at Monash University.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 104,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<h4>English Language Requirements</h4><p><strong>Part 1:</strong></p> <ul> <li>IELTS (or equivalent): 5.5 (no band lower than 5.0)</li> <li>TOEFL Internet based score: 52 – Writing 15, Speaking 14, Listening and Reading 5</li> </ul> <p><strong>Part 2:</strong></p> <ul> <li>IELTS (or equivalent): 6.0 (Writing not less than 6.0, no other band less than 5.5)</li> <li>TOEFL Internet based score: 69 – Writing 21, Speaking 14, Listening and Reading 5</li> </ul>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/U0602',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '116953J';

