UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4> <p>You will undertake your doctoral studies through the <a href="http://www.monash.edu/migr/future-students/phd">Monash doctoral program</a>. At the core of this program is the completion of a substantial research thesis on an agreed topic under the guidance of a supervisory team. Your research training is further enhanced by professional development activities or coursework units designed to support you in your academic and professional development.</p> <p>Upon completion of your doctoral studies at Monash, you will be able to demonstrate that you have successfully designed and executed a research project that makes an original and substantial contribution to your discipline. In your research journey, you will have also acquired the necessary skills and professional attributes to make an immediate and enduring impact on academia, industry, government, or community.</p> <h4>Structure</h4><p><strong>This course consists of:</strong></p> <ul><li>a creative practice project and a written exegesis</li></ul> <p>You must, in consultation with and under the direct supervision of a member/s of Faculty academic staff:</p> <ol><li>carry out a program of research on an agreed topic approved by the school or centre of enrolment in your chosen discipline for a specified period, including attending and/or presenting at seminars and other related activities as indicated by the relevant School;</li><li>submit, for examination, a dissertation component which meets the requirements of the examiners consisting of:</li></ol> <p> i) a written exegesis <strong>of a minimum of 30,000 words</strong> that articulates the conceptual, methodological and aesthetic context for the creative works; <strong>AND</strong></p> <p> ii)<strong> one of the following practice-based creative components</strong> in line with disciplinary requirements.</p> <p><strong>Specialisations are available in the following disciplines:</strong></p> <p><strong>Creative Writing</strong></p> <p>A major creative work of a minimum of 40,000 words which may include one or more of the following:</p> <ul><li>a novel;</li><li>a group of short stories;</li><li>a work of creative non-fiction;</li><li>a play or group of plays;</li><li>a sequence of poems (book length, approximately 100 pages);</li><li>or a portfolio of works of various genres.</li></ul> <p><strong>Translation Studies </strong></p> <p>A body of translated work of a minimum of 40,000 words. This may include:</p> <ul><li>a novel or a group of short stories;</li><li>a play or group of plays;</li><li>a sequence of poems;</li><li>translation of an audio-visual work or video game; or,</li><li>a portfolio of works of various genres</li></ul> <p><strong>Journalism</strong></p> <p>A body of work whose length depends upon the project and genre and will be negotiated with supervisors. This may include:</p> <ul><li>a full-length book manuscript of journalism of a minimum of 40,000 words;</li><li>a full-length book manuscript of creative non-fiction of a minimum of 40,000</li><li>words;</li><li>a full-length audio journalism documentary of up to 60 minutes;</li><li>a series of two to five linked radio or podcast productions of up to 25 minutes</li><li>each that constitutes a coherent body of work;</li><li>a short to mid-length fiction film of up to 50 minutes;</li><li>a full-length video journalism documentary of up to 90 minutes;</li><li>a series of three to five linked video productions of up to 20 minutes each that</li><li>constitutes a coherent body of work;</li><li>multimedia journalism that represents a substantial effort in the genre;</li><li>a feature-length audiovisual script or screenplay of 50 to 100 pages;</li><li>an essay film or audiovisual essay of 50 to 90 minutes;</li><li>a series of three to four short (three- to 10-minute) VR or AR projects;</li><li>a presentation of audiovisual technical experiments that explore the</li><li>boundaries of new or existing media technologies.</li></ul> <p><strong>Music</strong>:</p> <ul><li>a live presentation of approximately 70 to 90 minutes in duration; OR</li><li>a portfolio consisting of an equivalent duration in music scores, recordings, programming outcomes, video or other media as appropriate to the discipline.</li></ul> <p><strong>Theatre Performance</strong></p> <ul><li>A live presentation of approximately 60-90 minutes in duration, which is presented at a Monash University venue or an approved alternative. OR</li><li>a portfolio consisting of an equivalent duration in performances (live or recorded), performance texts, video or other media as appropriate to the discipline.</li></ul> <p><strong>The total combined thesis components must not exceed 100,000 words.</strong></p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Must meet Monash University doctoral English Language Proficiency requirements</li><li>If first language not English, satisfy ELP via one of:</li><li>Language of Instruction: completed tertiary course within last 2 years, ≥2 years duration, taught and assessed entirely in English, from an approved English language country, any HE institution where English is sole language of instruction (WHED), Monash Malaysia or Indonesia campus, or a two‑year Monash‑Southeast University Double Masters coursework</li><li>English Language Test (taken at official centre within 2 years of start): IELTS Academic 7.0 overall (no band <6.5); TOEFL paper‑based 587+ TWE 4.5; TOEFL iBT 94+ (Reading 19, Listening 20, Speaking 20, Writing 24); Pearson Test of Academic English 65+ communicative 58; C1 Advanced 185 overall (Writing 185, no skill below 169)</li><li>Submit full documentary evidence of ELP with application; Monash may request additional evidence</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/A8001',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '117341G';

-- ⚠️ Skipped (no/unreliable CRICOS course code): Doctor of Physiotherapy | https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/M6032

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>In this course, you will develop the relevant knowledge, practical and analytical skills required for podiatry practice, as well as extend your undergraduate skills of communication, ethical and reflective practice, collaboration, research, and leadership. This graduate entry course is delivered over three years. Foundational knowledge and skills are introduced in year one, with the focus on integration of prior science knowledge and principles into the context of podiatry in addition to the development of clinical skills. An integrated and case-based learning approach facilitates active learning and development, which include early workplace and interprofessional learning opportunities. Clinical placements occur in years two and three, with practice in a simulated setting prior to acute, subacute, community and private practice clinical placements. A learner-centred philosophy allows preferences to be pursued in an independent research project in the final years.</p> <p>This accelerated course enables students with an undergraduate degree in foundational sciences to obtain an entry-level qualification at a postgraduate level of study to practice as a podiatrist. The course includes clinical education that satisfies Podiatry Board of Australia (Ahpra) requirements for registration as a podiatrist with registration endorsed for use of scheduled medicines. This course provides an integrated curriculum, centred on case-based learning to be practice ready.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The integrated curriculum is delivered over three years and develops across five themes: personal and professional development; population, society and health; fundamental knowledge for working as a podiatrist within the biopsychosocial model of health sciences; applied practice; and research. These themes are interwoven throughout the course.</p> <p><strong>Personal and professional development</strong><br/>This theme develops the personal attributes and skills required to make the transition to becoming a podiatrist. These include verbal and written communication skills, reflective practice, ethical and legal knowledge and leadership skills required to practice as a podiatrist and within an interprofessional team.</p> <p><strong>Population and society</strong> <br/>This theme addresses the broader society and population context and issues that impact upon podiatry practice, underpinned by an internationally accepted biopsychosocial health model.</p> <p><strong>Fundamental knowledge</strong> <br/>This theme provides the knowledge and skills required for podiatry practice. It includes contextualisation of previous anatomy, pharmacology, and behavioural sciences knowledge to clinical presentations as well as the theory informing podiatry, education and leadership practice.</p> <p><strong>Applied practice</strong><br/>This theme addresses clinical competencies for podiatry practice. It focuses on the incorporation of the best available research evidence with the clinical reasoning skills of assessment, management, evaluation and health care across the lifespan and across a spectrum of health care environments and circumstances.</p> <p><strong>Research</strong><br/>This theme will develop knowledge and skills to locate, interpret and critically evaluate research relevant to podiatry practice. An independent research project relevant to the podiatry profession will be completed.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong><br/>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/students/handbooks/maps/2026/map-m6043.pdf">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 156,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td>Monash Level C, that is: IELTS (Academic): 7.0 overall (no band lower than 6.5); or Pearson Test of English (Academic): score of 65 overall with no band lower than 58; or TOEFL Internet-based test: score of 94 overall with minimum scores: Writing: 24, Listening: 20, Reading: 19 and Speaking: 20; or Equivalent approved English test</td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/M6043',
    updated_at = NOW()
