UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>The Master of Business Information Systems is a postgraduate degree that prepares you for a career in an information systems field such as business/system analyst, IT/IS consultant, IT product/project manager, and information manager.</p> <p>The rapidly changing business and technology landscape entails constant digital innovations. Offered by the Faculty of IT (the only faculty within Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight universities dedicated to IT), MBIS is specifically designed to enhance and update your knowledge and skills on the emerging technology and their business applications, the design and management of digital innovations, as well as the socio-technical dynamics of changes.</p> <p>MBIS is distinctive in its holistic emphasis on two key pillars, namely <em>strategic, transformational and ethical information systems</em>, and <em>information systems innovation, design and analytics</em>.</p> <p>The course will offer state-of-the-art conceptual frameworks to make sense of digital innovation and disruptions, as well as the practical tools and techniques for hands-on application to complex problems. The course offers an industry experience project where you have the opportunity to interact with experienced IT professionals, and work in a multidisciplinary team with other postgraduate students from the Faculty of IT. The course also offers a research-oriented option where you complete a minor-thesis research project.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course is structured in three part: Part A. Foundations for advanced business information systems, Part B. Core master''s study and Part C. Advanced practice. All students complete Part B and Part C.<br/><br/><strong>Part A. Foundation studies</strong><br/><br/>These units provide an introduction to information systems and business concepts, including system design and analysis as applied in professional practice. You will learn basic software programming and development concepts and database technology.</p> <p>These units are intended for students whose previous qualification is not in a cognate field. <br/><br/><strong>Part B. Core studies</strong></p> <p>These units draw on best practices within business information systems (BIS) to gain critical understanding of the theories and practice relating to responsible digitalisation, digital transformation, BIS strategy, governance and management, ethical BIS innovation and design, business intelligence and analytics, project management, as well as a choice of data and archive, and business process and decision-making behaviour.</p> <p><strong>Part C. Applied studies</strong></p> <p>The focus of these studies is professional or scholarly work in the broad realm of business information systems. <br/><br/>You have two options:</p> <ul><li>a program of coursework involving advanced study and an industry experience studio project.</li><li>a research pathway including a thesis. If you wish to use this master''s course as a pathway to a higher degree by research you should take this option.</li></ul> <p><strong>Master''s entry points</strong></p> <p>Depending on prior qualifications you may receive entry level credit which determines your point of entry to the course:</p> <ul><li>If you are admitted at entry level 1 you complete 96 credit points, comprising Part A, Part B and Part C.</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 2 you complete 72 credit points, comprising Part B and Part C.</li></ul> <p>Note: If you are eligible for credit for prior studies you may elect not to receive the credit and complete the higher credit-point option.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The course progression map (<a href="https://www.monash.edu/it/current-students/enrolment/course-maps-and-handbooks">Clayton</a>) (<a href="https://www.monash.edu.my/it/current-students/postgraduates/course-maps">Malaysia</a>) provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 104,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level A: IELTS (Academic) 6.5 overall, no band lower than 6.0</li><li>Monash Level A: Pearson Test of English (Academic) score 58 overall, no band lower than 50</li><li>Monash Level A: TOEFL iBT score 79 overall with minimum Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18</li><li>Monash Level A: Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/C6003',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '079053A';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Structure</h4><p>Double degree courses include all the features of the component degree courses, except that electives may be reduced or redirected.</p> <p><strong>Master of Business Information Systems</strong></p> <p>Master of Business Information Systems is structured in three parts, Part A. Foundation studies, Part B. Core studies and Part C. Applied studies. In the double degree course you complete: </p> <p><strong>Part A. Foundation studies </strong></p> <p>These units provide an introduction to information systems and business concepts, including system design and analysis as applied in professional practice. You will learn basic software programming and development concepts and database technology.</p> <p>These units are intended for students whose previous qualification is not in a cognate field.</p> <p><strong>Part B. Core studies </strong></p> <p>These units draw on best practices within business information systems (BIS) to gain critical understanding of the theories and practice relating to responsible digitalisation, digital transformation, BIS strategy, governance and management, ethical BIS innovation and design, business intelligence and analytics, project management, as well as a choice of data and archive, and business process and decision-making behaviour..</p> <p><strong>Part C. Applied studies</strong></p> <p>The focus of these studies is professional or scholarly work in the broad realm of business information systems.</p> <p><strong>Master of Global Business</strong></p> <p>The Master of Global Business is structured in two parts, Part A. Core studies, Part B. Capstone studies and Part C. Electives. In the double degree course you complete:</p> <p><strong>Part A. Core studies</strong></p> <p>The focus of these studies is to develop your knowledge and skills as a critical and creative professional who can apply their understanding and knowledge of the global business and politics environments in order to contribute to solutions that address contemporary global issues.</p> <p><strong>Master’s entry points</strong></p> <p>Depending on prior qualifications you may receive entry level credit which determines your point of entry to the course:</p> <ul><li>If you are admitted at entry level 1, you complete 120 credit points, comprising Part A, Part B. and Part C. of the Master of Business Information Systems and Part A. of the Master of Global Business.</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 2, you complete 96 credit points, comprising Part B and Part C. of the Master of Business Information Systems and Part A. of the Master of Global Business.</li></ul> <p>Note: If you are eligible for credit for prior studies you may elect not to receive the credit and complete the higher credit-point option.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong><br/>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/it/current-students/enrolment/course-maps-and-handbooks">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 130,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
