UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>The Master of Commerce provides pre-doctoral training for high-achieving students who intend to pursue a Monash PhD. The course comprises advanced coursework and research methodology training designed to prepare you to undertake deep and effective doctoral research when you transition to the PhD. During the course, you will design and undertake independent research in your specialisation, working closely with a supervisor who will provide individual guidance and academic advice.</p> <p>The Master of Commerce is the <strong>compulsory </strong>doctoral pathway for the Banking and finance specialisation.</p> <p>The Master of Commerce is the <strong>preferred </strong>doctoral pathway for the following specialisations:</p> <ul> <li>Accounting</li> <li>Econometrics and business statistics</li> <li>Economics</li> <li>Health economics</li> <li>Management</li> <li>Marketing studies</li> </ul> <p>Upon successful completion of the Master of Commerce, students may progress to the PhD program in the relevant discipline, subject to meeting selection standards. </p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course is structured in two parts: Part A. Research studies and Part B. Advanced research studies.</p> <p><strong>Part A. Core research studies</strong></p> <p>These studies will enable you to develop a deep understanding of the latest research approaches within the discipline and develop your skills in conducting research on complex business issues.</p> <p><strong>Part B. Advanced research studies</strong></p> <p>These units will equip you with foundational expertise and advanced theoretical and methodological knowledge necessary for successful doctoral research within the discipline, with the opportunity to work with a range of academic staff to further develop your research skills and aspirations.</p> <p><strong>Master''s entry points</strong></p> <p>Depending on prior qualifications you may receive entry level credit (a form of block credit) which determines your point of entry to the course:</p> <ul><li>If you are admitted at entry level 1 you must complete 96 credit points, comprising Part A and Part B.</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 2 you must complete 48 credit points, comprising Part B.</li></ul> <p><strong>Note:</strong></p> <p>If you are eligible for credit for prior studies you may elect not to receive the credit and complete one of the higher credit-point options.</p> <p>For Semester 2 commencement, only applicants who meet the Entry Level 2 requirements will be considered.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/business/current-students/course-advice-and-planning/helpful-links/course-maps/2026/graduate">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 104,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level A: IELTS (Academic) 6.5 overall, no band lower than 6.0</li><li>Monash Level A: Pearson Test of English (Academic) score 58 overall, no band lower than 50</li><li>Monash Level A: TOEFL iBT score 79 overall with minimum Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18</li><li>Monash Level A: Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/B6023',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '0101330';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>There has never been a more important or exciting time to study the mediated world.</p> <p>This course builds your understanding of the changing contexts of media and communication practices. You will gain insight into the key issues affecting the communications and media industries and you will examine these developments from cultural, political and theoretical perspectives. You will be challenged to communicate your new knowledge and insights with impact and purpose, accommodating a range of media formats and potential audiences.</p> <p>This course develops an advanced understanding of the contemporary communications and media environment. It is suitable for those pursuing careers in a wide range of communications and media roles, including in the areas of communications, media relations, social media, digital engagement, public policy and public relations.</p> <p>You will have the opportunity to engage with local practitioners and industry experts via guest speakers, case studies, internships and site visits. You will also have the opportunity to conduct in-depth analysis of contemporary media industries, issues and organisations.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course is structured in five parts: Part A. Foundation studies, Part B. Core studies, Part C. Applied studies, Part D. Professional practice studies and Part E. Elective studies.</p> <p>A research pathway is also available subject to meeting eligibility requirements.</p> <p><strong>Part A. Foundation studies</strong></p> <p>These studies provide you with an introduction to communications and media studies.</p> <p><strong>Part B. Core studies</strong></p> <p>These studies draw on best practices within the field of communications and media studies to further your understanding of communications and media industries, issues and practices, both locally and globally. Studies focus on the challenges posed by digital disruption, globalisation and on methods of research and analysis for the contemporary media and communications environment.</p> <p><strong>Part C. Applied studies</strong></p> <p>The focus of these studies is professional or scholarly work that can contribute to a portfolio of professional development. </p> <p><strong>Part D. Professional practice studies</strong></p> <p>Designed in association with industry experts, select from this suite of professional enhancement offerings to build essential competencies for your career pathway.</p> <p><strong>Part E. Elective studies</strong></p> <p>These studies provide you with the opportunity to undertake a program of coursework study tailored to your own interests. Units not taken in Part B, Part C and Part D are also available as elective options.</p> <p><strong>Master''s entry points</strong></p> <p>Depending on prior qualifications you may receive entry level credit (a form of block credit) which determines your point of entry to the course:</p> <ul><li>If you are admitted at entry level 1 you complete 96 credit points, comprising Part A, Part B, Part C, Part D, and Part E.</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 2 you complete 72 credit points (Caulfield Campus), comprising Part B, Part C, Part D (6 credit points) and Part E (30 credit points).</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 2 you complete 72 credit points (Malaysia Campus), comprising Part B, Part C, Part D (6 credit points) and Part E (30 credit points).</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 3 you complete 48 credit points, comprising Part B, Part C and Part E (12 credit points).</li></ul> <p>Note: If you are enrolled at the Malaysian campus you are admitted at entry level 2 or 3.</p> <p>Note: If you are eligible for credit for prior studies you may elect not to receive the credit and complete one of the higher credit-point options.</p> <p><strong>Course progression maps</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/arts/course-information/maps/">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p> <p>If you are eligible to enrol in the 1.5-year entry point, you have the option of completing this course intensively over a 12-month period by following the <a href="https://www.monash.edu/arts/course-information/maps/">intensive course progression map</a>.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 104,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>IELTS (Academic) overall 6.5 with no band lower than 6.0</li><li>Pearson Test of English (Academic) overall 58 with no band lower than 50</li><li>TOEFL iBT overall 79 with minimum Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18</li><li>Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/A6003',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '082653F';

