UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>Designed to advance your educational knowledge and expertise to enable local and global educational change, the Master of Education provides you with the opportunity to engage with critical disciplinary issues and contemporary debates to develop the capacity to have impact. Selecting from a choice of specialisations and unique pathways that will suit educators from a range of sectors including early childhood, schools, higher education, workplaces and the community, you will have the opportunity to tailor your learning experience to best facilitate your learning objectives and needs. </p> <p>Intended to cultivate critical thinking, analytical and creative problem-solving capabilities, you will learn in a supportive environment where you will work with your peers and leading experts in education. In this context, you will be challenged to apply your new knowledge and skills to real world problems. By selecting a pre-defined specialisation in early childhood education, digital learning, general education studies, inclusive and special education, STEM education^, social justice^ or mathematics leadership*, you will focus on the concepts, issues and learnings particular to that specialisation. While the general education specialisation will provide you with an opportunity to engage with a range of contemporary educational areas.</p> <p>The course is mindful of the busy professional lives and diverse needs and expertise of our learners. Consequently, you will have access to a range of flexibly taught units, while undertaking the course either full-time or part-time.</p> <p>This course also offers the opportunity to expand your learning into a higher degree by research.<br/><br/>Note: This course does not provide a recognised initial teaching qualification. If you are seeking an initial teaching qualification, you should refer to the information on the Faculty of Education''s <a href="https://handbook.monash.edu/current/courses/D6001">Master of Teaching</a> course.</p> <p>*The mathematics leadership specialisation is offered on a cohort basis only and is not available in 2025.</p> <p>^The STEM education and Social justice specialisations are not available in 2025. </p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course is structured in three parts: Part A. Orientation to education, Part B. Expert studies in education and Part C. Professional inquiry. </p> <p><strong>Part A. Orientation to education</strong><br/><br/>These studies will introduce you to contemporary issues in the study of education. They are intended for students who come into the course without previous qualifications in education or work experience in education-related fields. You will engage with academic traditions and scholarly practices in the field of education at the postgraduate level, become acquainted with a broad range of debates across education contexts and develop an understanding of the complexities evident in the field. <br/><br/><strong>Part B. Expert studies in education</strong></p> <p>These studies will allow you to extend your knowledge, skills and proficiency within your chosen specialisation area. You will focus on the key theoretical frameworks and concepts particular to your field of interest, as you engage with critical disciplinary issues and contemporary debates. The expertise which you develop within your specialisation, will equip you to make contextually confident decisions that speak to the needs of a contemporary workplace.</p> <p><strong>Part C. Professional inquiry</strong></p> <p>These studies provide you with an opportunity to undertake a program of coursework study which you can tailor to your own interests. This may include selecting a sequence of units in an identified area or selecting units from a broad cross section of educational interests.</p> <p><strong>Master''s entry points</strong></p> <p>Depending on prior qualifications you may receive entry level credit (a form of block credit) which determines your point of entry to the course:</p> <ul><li>If you are admitted at entry level 1 you will complete 96 credit points, comprising Part A, Part B and Part C</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 2 you will complete 72 credit points, comprising Part B and Part C</li><li>If you are admitted at entry level 3 you will complete 48 credit points, comprising Part B</li></ul> <p>Note: If you are eligible for credit for prior studies, you may elect not to receive the credit and complete one of the higher credit-point options.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/education/students/courses/maps/2026">course progression maps</a> provide guidance on unit enrolment for each specialisation.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 104,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>Program</strong></td><td>Monash English Bridging (MEB) program offered by Monash College for students who meet Monash academic requirements but narrowly miss English language requirements</td></tr><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>MEB - Standard (Level A)</li><li>Courses requiring IELTS 6.5 (no band less than 6.0)</li><li>10, 15 or 20 week program</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><strong>English Language Requirements</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level A: IELTS Academic 6.5 overall (no band lower than 6.0)</li><li>Pearson Test of English Academic score 58 overall (no band lower than 50)</li><li>TOEFL iBT score 79 overall with minimum Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18</li><li>Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/D6002',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '078862J';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>This course will prepare you to practise as an educational and developmental psychologist in a range of settings including primary and secondary schools, health and welfare services, hospitals, care facilities, clinics and private practice and within business and corporate environments. <br/><br/>This course aims to develop you as an evidence-based practitioner and an independent professional who has the theoretical, research, assessment and therapeutic skills required in educational and developmental psychology. You will develop an advanced ability to think critically and analytically about the processes involved in educational and developmental psychology and to apply them in professional practice and in research. This course is accredited by the <a href="https://www.psychologycouncil.org.au/">Australian Psychology Accreditation Council</a> (APAC) as a fifth and sixth year sequence in psychology (APAC levels 3 and 4). </p><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course is structured in two parts: Part A. Applied studies and Part B. Clinical placement.</p> <p><strong>Part A. Applied studies</strong><br/><br/>These studies will advance your knowledge and skill development for psychology practice. Guided by sound ethical principles, and through collaborative participation in coursework lectures and workshops, you will develop both expert knowledge of psychology across the lifespan and your critical thinking skills for professional practice.</p> <p>During your first year you will also undertake 200 hours of practical activities and professional experience across the year.</p> <p>As a developing scientist-practitioner, you will review empirical literature and conduct scientific investigations relevant to the theory and practice of educational and developmental psychology. This will culminate in the reporting of an independent empirical investigation in the form of a 6,000 to 8,000 word research thesis. <br/><br/><strong>Part B. Clinical placement</strong><br/><br/>These studies involve professional experience across a variety of settings where you have the opportunity to apply theory to practice under the supervision of experienced specialist practitioners. Across the course you will complete a minimum of 1000 recorded hours of supervised professional experience.</p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/education/students/courses/maps/2026">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 104,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level D – IELTS Academic: overall 7.0 with no band below 7.0</li><li>Monash Level D – Pearson Test of English Academic: overall 65 with no band below 65</li><li>Monash Level D – TOEFL iBT: overall 98 with minimum scores – Writing 27, Listening 24, Reading 24, Speaking 23</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/D6007',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '038585A';

-- ⚠️ Skipped (no/unreliable CRICOS course code): Master of Educational and Developmental Psychology Advanced | https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/D6016

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>This course combines the coursework and placement requirements of the Master of Educational and Developmental Psychology with the thesis requirements of the PhD. It is an <a href="https://www.psychologycouncil.org.au/">Australian Psychology Accreditation Council</a> (APAC) accredited fifth and sixth year sequence in psychology.</p> <p>Enrolment will be probationary with full admission to the course granted in the last three months of the second year of full-time study, or equivalent for part-time study, if performance on all components, including research confirmation proceedings, is satisfactory.</p> <p>The course aims to develop you as an evidence-based practitioner and an independent specialist professional who has the theoretical, research, assessment and therapeutic skills required in educational and developmental psychology. You will develop an advanced ability to think critically and analytically about the processes involved in educational and developmental psychology and apply them in professional practice and research.</p> <p>In undertaking the PhD research thesis, you will develop as a scientist-practitioner, carrying out reviews and scientific investigations which make original and substantive contributions relevant to educational and developmental psychology.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p><strong>This course consists of:</strong></p> <p>a. applied academic studies in psychology</p> <p>b. clinical placements in psychology</p> <p>c. a research thesis.</p> <p>You will enrol for year one in the Master of Educational and Developmental Psychology. This requires completion of the initial 48 credit points of the combined degree consisting of eight advanced coursework units where you will commence preparation of your thesis research proposal. The first professional experience units are undertaken in the first year.</p> <p>Upon satisfactory completion of the first 48 credit points, you will enrol in the research intensive component in the Master of Educational and Developmental Psychology and Doctor of Philosophy. In year two, you will complete the remaining two practicum units, further develop and present your research proposal, finalise ethical clearance and progress the research proposal towards confirmation that takes place in the last three months of the year. Enrolment for the final two years is dependent on satisfying the confirmation process.</p> <p>If you are a part-time student, you are enrolled pro-rata. Coursework occurs in year 1 and 2 of MEDP, confirmation would therefore only occur in year 3 or 4 of the combined course, after coursework is completed and students have articulated to the PhD.</p> <p>Units are 6 credit points unless otherwise stated.</p> <p><strong>Coursework component</strong></p> <p><strong>Advanced coursework units</strong></p> <ul><li>EDF5561 Clinical case formulation</li><li>EDF5562 Ethics for professional practice</li><li>EDF5563 Cognitive assessment</li><li>EDF5568 Professional experience in psychology</li><li>EDF5569 Advanced practice in educational and developmental psychology</li><li>EDF5570 Advanced clinical interventions for mental health</li><li>EDF5571 Advanced psychological assessment and diagnosis</li><li>EDF5572 Professional experience: Working with systems and groups</li></ul> <p><strong>Practicum units</strong></p> <ul><li>EDF5732 Clinical placement in psychology 2 (12 points)</li><li>EDF5733 Clinical placement in psychology 3 (12 points)</li></ul> <p><strong>Research component</strong></p> <p>As a student of the university, you will be required to abide by all relevant statutes, regulations, policies and procedures. This includes maintaining satisfactory progress via regular milestone reviews in accordance with the <a href="http://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/787311/Graduate-Research-Progress-Management-Policy.pdf">Graduate Research Progress Management policy</a> and procedures, ensuring you are undertaking research of an appropriate quality and scale as required by your course. You should refer to the University''s current statutes and the <a href="https://www.monash.edu/policy-bank/academic/graduate-education">University Policy Bank</a> for links to relevant policies, procedures and guidelines.</p> <p>You must, in consultation with and under the direct supervision of a member/s of the academic staff:</p> <p>1. carry out a program of research on an agreed topic approved by the faculty for a specified period, including attending and/or presenting at seminars and other related activities as indicated by the relevant department/school</p> <p>2. submit for assessment a thesis of not more than 80,000 words on the program of research which meets the requirements of the examiners. Submission of a <a href="https://www.monash.edu/graduate-research/supervisors-and-examiners/examiners/publication">thesis including published works</a> may be permitted.</p> <p>In addition to the coursework grades required for admission to the Master of Educational and Developmental Psychology/PhD, you must pass all coursework units, the assessment of which is conducted in accordance with the University''s <a href="http://policy.monash.edu.au/policy-bank/academic/education/assessment/assessment-in-coursework-units-policy.html">Assessment in Coursework Units policy</a> and supporting procedures.</p> <p><strong>Research areas</strong></p> <p>A wide variety of thesis topics related to educational and developmental psychology across the lifespan are possible, subject to available supervision. Refer to the <a href="https://www.monash.edu/education/about/schools-and-centres/educational-psychology-and-counselling#tabs__2771546-03">School of Educational Psychology and Counselling</a> for the range of research topics. Contact the faculty''s <a href="http://www.monash.edu.au/education/research/degrees/contact/">Research Degrees Office</a> for further information.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<h4>English Language Requirements</h4><p>Students will have to meet the English Language Proficiency requirements for a doctoral degree at Monash University. Please refer to the <a href="https://www.monash.edu/graduate-research/support-and-resources/handbooks">website</a> for detailed information.</p> <p>Full documentary evidence in support of English Language Proficiency (ELP) must be submitted with applications for admission and scholarship. Monash University reserves the right to seek further documentary evidence of ELP. </p>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/3736',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '083826D';

