UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>The Master of Engineering is designed for graduates who already have an undergraduate degree in engineering in a cognate discipline. This course is designed to extend your knowledge in your chosen specialisation area and advance your leadership and complex problem-solving skills in a cross-cultural environment. It is available in the following specialisations:</p> <ul> <li>Engineering management </li> <li>Biological engineering</li> <li>Civil engineering </li> <li>Electrical engineering</li> <li>Energy transitions engineering</li> <li>Materials engineering</li> <li>Mechanical engineering</li> <li>Power systems engineering</li> <li>Renewable energy engineering</li> <li>Smart manufacturing engineering</li> </ul><h4>Structure</h4><p>The course comprises 48 points structured into three parts: Part A. Core studies, Part B. Specialist studies, and Part C. Specialist elective studies. </p> <p><strong>Part A. Core studies</strong><br/>These units are designed to establish IT literacy and develop essential skills that translate theory into engineering practice and research. <br/><br/><strong>Part B. Specialist studies</strong><br/>In this part, you will identify, interpret and critically appraise current developments and advanced technologies to synthesise evidence-based design solutions within your specialisation.<br/><br/><strong>Part C. Specialist elective studies</strong><br/>These units are designed to provide breadth, either outside of the chosen engineering specialisation and/or in disciplines allied with and supportive of engineering. </p> <p><strong>Course progression map</strong></p> <p>The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/4079121/2026-map-E6014.pdf">course progression map</a> provides guidance on unit enrolment for each semester of study.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 52,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>Monash Level A: IELTS (Academic) 6.5 overall, no band lower than 6.0</li><li>Monash Level A: Pearson Test of English (Academic) score 58 overall, no band lower than 50</li><li>Monash Level A: TOEFL iBT score 79 overall with minimum Writing 21, Listening 12, Reading 13, Speaking 18</li><li>Monash Level A: Equivalent approved English test</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/E6014',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '104589A';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4><p>This is a joint master''s by research program between Monash University and Southeast University. You will undertake your master''s studies through a collaborative arrangement between Monash University and Southeast University. </p> <p>Enrolment in a master''s by research degree involves the independent investigation of a research problem that has been formulated by you as the student. It is expected that the research you undertake will make a contribution to the discipline in which you are enrolled by applying, critiquing, analysing or interpreting that knowledge in ways that facilitate pathways for further learning. You will be supported in your research by a supervisory team.</p> <p>Whilst it is expected that your program is based at your home University, you will also be required to spend a minimum of 8 months at the host University.</p><h4>Structure</h4><p>This course consists of a research and thesis component.</p> <p>You must, in consultation with and under the direct supervision of a member/s of the academic staff:</p> <ol><li>carry out a program of research on an agreed topic approved by the faculty in your chosen discipline for a specified period, including attending and/or presenting at seminars and other related activities as indicated by the faculty; and</li><li>submit for assessment a thesis of not more than 50,000 words on the program of research which meets the requirements of the examiners.</li></ol> <p>Submission of a <a href="https://www.monash.edu/graduate-research/supervisors-and-examiners/examiners/publication">thesis including published works</a> may be permitted.</p> <p><strong>AREAS OF RESEARCH</strong></p> <p><strong>Chemical and Biological engineering</strong></p> <p>Biofuels, bioprocessing and biomaterials, colloid and interface science, combustion and gasification, corrosion, food and pharmaceutical engineering, green chemicals, nanotechnology, mesoporous materials and membranes, powder technology, reaction engineering, rheology and sustainable processing.</p> <p><strong>Civil engineering</strong></p> <p>Geomechanics and geotechnical engineering, structural engineering, construction materials and management, digital twins and infrastructure monitoring, transport planning and traffic engineering, autonomous transport systems, water resources engineering, remote sensing, environmental engineering, mining and resources engineering, renewable and geothermal energy systems, computational modelling and simulation.</p> <p><strong>Electrical and computer systems engineering</strong></p> <p>Computer systems and software engineering, control engineering, electronics, telecommunications engineering, electrical power systems, bio-optics, biomedical engineering, plasmonics, nanophotonics, optical physics and robotics.</p> <p><strong>Materials science and engineering</strong></p> <p>Engineering alloys &amp; processing, additive manufacturing, phase transformations &amp; microstructure–property relationships, mechanical, corrosion &amp; behaviour modelling, advanced polymers, composites &amp; surface properties, polymer chemistry &amp; processing, biomaterials, tissue engineering, 3D bioprinting, scaffolds, drug delivery &amp; antimicrobial coatings, functional, energy, photovoltaic, 2D, magnetic &amp; spintronic materials, materials characterisation, modelling &amp; theory, advanced microscopy, synchrotron &amp; neutron methods, atomic structure analysis, computational materials science, ceramics, nanomaterials, surface/interface design, electrospun &amp; melt-spun films, aerospace &amp; defence materials, nuclear materials, sovereign capability development.</p> <p><strong>Mechanical and Aerospace engineering</strong></p> <p>Aerodynamics, fluid mechanics, turbulence, structural mechanics, aerospace engineering, composites, heat transfer, micro/nano fluid and solid mechanics, robotics, mechatronics, railway engineering and maintenance engineering.</p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 104,
    offshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td>Sound English language skills are required for academic programs and to meet the English Language Proficiency requirements for a research master''s degree at Monash University. For more information, visit the university webpage.</td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://handbook.monash.edu/2026/courses/E7001',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '110789J';

